// ADR-020 §4 (public, book-gated) + §3.1.1: `Some(NaN)` survives a nullable
// position — a NaN is an ordinary `number` value, not an absence signal.
//
// This fence tests SEMANTICS, not a carrier, and that is deliberate: at HEAD
// `number?` takes the `Ptr(HeapKind::Option)` heap carrier, while the §3.1
// NaN-sentinel encoding (`StorageType::NullableFloat64`) has no producer yet
// and is #229's landing target. The assertion below holds under BOTH, so it
// keeps gating after #229 migrates `Option<scalar>` off the heap carrier.
//
// It only became writable once #225b made float division IEEE-754: before
// that the VM raised on `0.0/0.0` and a NaN could not be constructed at all.
//
// NaN PAYLOAD bits are UNSPECIFIED through nullable positions. Today's carrier
// preserves them; the §3.1 encoding canonicalizes them. Shape exposes no float
// bit introspection, so neither is observable — and "unspecified" is the only
// claim both carriers keep true.
fn dv(a: number, b: number) -> number { return a / b }
fn wrap(x: number) -> number? { return Some(x) }

// A computed NaN inside Some() is a value, so `??` does NOT take the default.
print(wrap(dv(0.0, 0.0)) ?? -1.0)
// Infinities are ordinary values too.
print(wrap(dv(1.0, 0.0)) ?? -1.0)
print(wrap(dv(0.0 - 1.0, 0.0)) ?? -1.0)
// An ordinary finite number is unaffected.
print(wrap(2.5) ?? -1.0)
